<p>Cuba said it will allow Cuban nationals living abroad to invest in the island’s private sector and own businesses there, a notable policy shift as the country faces a severe economic crisis marked by fuel shortages and power problems. Officials also said Havana wants to ease obstacles for U.S. companies and other foreign investors, signaling a broader push to attract capital and revive commercial activity. The move stands out because Cuba has long kept tight limits on private enterprise and on the economic role of its diaspora, especially in the United States. Deputy Prime Minister Oscar Pérez-Oliva Fraga tied the opening to the need for growth under difficult conditions and said U.S. sanctions are holding back recovery, while several reports noted that the policy comes amid renewed pressure from President Trump and after recent blackouts and unrest on the island.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/2RmUB4CeGM6xdp_8vG4Rg8SVJdgVL7_o-Vb5kj-vxHJ6iq2E7fArfmrp0BoA5a0EMn7tw5Wxixe8fmBUhovrut3W_f0kIbW678yUhOVznHi-uiw5LGVysHQ4JRZv6wMmCFGFdGEue0FmOq7ktDhHghxXMjVLdPpjVuyq8oQEbRzgS_7pbo_LKKye-3Cl3xc_O8fWlFbRuX0' alt='The Güinera neighborhood of Havana on Friday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Beyond investing: El País reported that the plan would let Cubans living abroad return and take a more direct role in the domestic economy, going beyond capital contributions alone.</li><li>Formal rollout: El País said the measure was expected to be unveiled as part of a broader government reform package meant to confront the island’s crisis.</li><li>Messenger matters: El País noted that the announcement came from Deputy Prime Minister Oscar Pérez-Oliva Fraga, a great-nephew of Fidel Castro, suggesting the outreach was being delivered by a senior insider.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuban government: Deputy Prime Minister Oscar Pérez-Oliva Fraga presented the policy as part of an effort to expand private-sector activity and said U.S. sanctions are blocking Cuba’s recovery. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-03-16-0700/world-cuba-will-allow-nationals-living-abroad-invest-and-own-businesses-island-economic-czar-tells-nbc-news'>NBC News via AllSides</a>)</li><li>El País analysis: El País described the opening as a significant shift in Havana’s relationship with its diaspora after years of restrictions, and as part of a broader reform package the government is expected to roll out. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-16/cuba-opens-up-to-investments-from-cubans-living-abroad.html'>El País</a>)</li><li>WSWS: WSWS argued that the measure could increase U.S. and exile-business influence over Cuba and described it as a strategic retreat under external pressure. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/03/16/rleo-m16.html'>World Socialist Web Site</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-03-16-0700/world-cuba-will-allow-nationals-living-abroad-invest-and-own-businesses-island-economic-czar-tells-nbc-news'>Cuba will allow nationals living abroad to invest in and own businesses on the island, economic czar tells NBC News</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-16/cuba-opens-up-to-investments-from-cubans-living-abroad.html'>Cuba opens up to investments from Cubans living abroad</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/16/world/americas/cuba-us-foreign-investment-businesses.html'>Cuba Ready to Accept Outside Investment, Top Official Says</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/3/17/cuba-tells-exiles-doors-are-open-to-invest-in-businesses-in-the-country'>Cuba tells exiles ‘doors are open’ to invest in businesses in the country</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/03/16/rleo-m16.html'>In major concession to Trump, Cuban government opens island to investment by Miami exile capitalists</a> - wsws.org</li><li><a href='https://